Z||000000||bHooper||10-08-1999||03:39 PM||||In a local paper there is a 88 Formula for sale.  They are asking $1000.  The ad reads: V-6 Fuel injection, AC, 5 speed, runs good, needs clutch, paint and door hinges, FC, one owner.<P>I may be wrong, but other than cosmetic changes, the Formula is mechanically (suspension/engine/tranny) identicall to the '88 GT's.  Correct?<P>I plan to look at this car on saturday, as I have a 86.5 gt that needs a 5 speed tranny, but would love to have the Lotus suspension  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||mwbackus||10-08-1999||04:28 PM||||     You have obviously stumbled upon one he!!ofa deal!  Thats about the same type of situation that I obtained mine from.  The owner does not want to spend the money for the clutch, and does not have the mechanical background to DIY.  Most dealers wont even look at a Fiero with a bad clutch as a tradein.  The poor guy probably has no idea what its really worth!  If the body and interior are in somewhat decent shape, and it runs ok, I would buy it in a heartbeat.  I could almost bet it only has around 100k miles on it!<BR>     Yes, the Formula does have the same suspension as the 88GT.  The "Formula" name as interpreted by Pontiac simply means a  performance version of that particular model.<BR>     Although there was an unsupported rumor that the 88's had a Lotus suspension, it was only a very misinterpreted concept.  The designers did study the Lotus (as they did many sports cars) during the development of the suspension, but it was in fact developed by Pontiac.  This was actually confirmed by Hulki Aldikacti, during a banquet at one of the many Fiero meets.<P>Mike||||reg||1
